uh now our next interview is the longest  serving health secretary or he was when  he handed over to matt hancock in 2018.  that's around 16 months before covert  hit  and when jeremy hunt stepped down from  front bench politics he said he wanted  to do for patient safety what al gore  did for climate change in a new book  he's called for fundamental culture  change in the nhs to prevent avoidable  deaths  and with questions over boris johnson's  future he's never ruled out another run  for the conservative leadership either  let's have a little listen to what  happened when we spoke to jeremy hunt  earlier this week i started by asking  about the nhs  well i think it can be a rogue system  from the point of view of families when  something goes wrong and sometimes they  feel the shutters come down and no one  wants to meet them and explain why a  loved one's died  but i also think it's important to say  that the vast majority of nhs care is  absolutely brilliant i've got three  lovely children all born on the nhs it  mended my elbow last year when i broke  it brilliant surgeon and physio  but the question is when things do go  wrong as they do in all health systems  how good are we at learning from  mistakes and what i found writing this  book is that the one thing the families  want when something goes wrong it's  never money it's just to feel that the  loss of their loved one has some meaning  because the system's learnt from what  what went wrong and  isn't going to make the same mistake  again and i guess that's the critical  point isn't it um not making the same  mistake again do you feel that the  shutters coming down as you say it has  effectively led to more avoidable deaths  yes and  when i was writing this book i came to  realize this is not just a problem in  the nhs it's a problem in healthcare  systems all over the world  but if you think about the most  traumatic thing that could happen in a  hospital which is perhaps a baby dying  that is equally traumatic for the  doctors and nurses and midwives  as it is for the family  and they have to come into work the next  day and and carry on looking after other  patients and they want to do nothing  more than be open and transparent and  learn the lessons  the system can make that impossible they  are worried frankly they'll get fired if  they are open about having made a  mistake but then they are thinking about  you know what are their bosses in the  nhs going to do the regulators what are  they going to say are they going to get  struck off the register  and  the result of that is that the one thing  that matters more than anything else  which is that lessons are learned  is the thing that often doesn't happen  because of this terrible blame culture  and i mean you speak a little bit as if  you  weren't health secretary i mean you were  the longest serving health secretary a  lot of people people would be thinking  why haven't you done something about it  well i try to be honest in this book  about where i did make progress and  where i failed to make progress  i think in terms of transparency and  openness we are in a better place  three million more patients were using  good or outstanding hospitals by the end  of my time compared to when i started  but there were also things i didn't fix  i didn't put in place a long-term plan  for the social care system and  you know when you hear stories as we've  been hearing this week about people  waiting an hour for an ambulance when  they have a stroke  a lot of the reason for that is because  hospitals can't discharge people into  the social care system and they get full  and so  there were things that i wish i had made  more progress on do you think boris  johnson should be making more progress  on social care as well i mean i remember  it's one of the things that he obviously  spoke about when he first became prime  minister and yet it doesn't feel like  much has happened  yes i think he has made some progress  the cap on care cost is being introduced  and that will be a big relief to  people who have  neurological conditions like dementia  but the core funding for local  authorities is still not where it needs  to be you know one of the stories that i  found most shocking recently is the  shrewsbury telford trust which the  report of course found that more than  200 babies and nine mothers died  unnecessarily because of failings in  that trust catastrophic failures in that  trust we also know of course that women  who aren't white are five times more  likely to die during pregnancy  is the maternity care system in the uk  fit for purpose  um the vast majority of births are  totally safe  but we can do better if we had the same  maternity safety rates of sweden a  thousand more babies would live every  year in england and why what's going  wrong is it funding for midwives what  what is it is attitudes well my select  committee did a report into this last  year  and donna ockendon did a report into  shrewsbury and telford  more recently and we both said the same  thing which is number one we need more  midwives and more doctors at least 2 000  more midwives at least 500 more doctors  and nurses and  other people  the government's accepted that  recommendation  the second thing though is have you seen  any progress on that or they just said  that they accept it  not yet what we've heard is the  government accepting the recommendation  but they haven't said when they will  recruit those extra midwives how long  ago was it that you scored for it then  we called for it last summer and  nothing's happened and we need to make  progress urgently because we need  every mum to know that  if they are going into a maternity unit  there isn't going to be a shortage of  midwives or doctors so it is absolutely  essential to act quickly do you think  that if men gave birth a system would  work better  it's a good question um i think there  are  blind spots when it comes to women's  illnesses talking about women's health  um there have been reports of coming  from the u.s that roe vs wade will be  overturned now you've personally said  before that you think the abortion limit  should be reduced to 12 weeks although  you have said that it's not a law that  you would introduce if you would be  prime minister it's just a personal  opinion of yours  what's your reaction to roe vs ways well  i'm totally against it let me  be clear i'm a supporter of the system  in holland  which has  no restrictions on early stage abortions  and then they get progressively  higher as the pregnancy continues so  that's the system that i  but it's a personal view and i  it's a matter of a free vote and i  always said in government positions this  is not something for me to pursue i'd be  extremely worried if in the united  states  we ended up with a situation where women  couldn't access  basic health rights  there's obviously been a huge pressure  on the nhs recently uh through covert  staff absences treatments being delayed  if there is a spike in cases in the  winter are you worried about the  preparedness of the nhs  i'm always worried about the capacity of  the nhs if there was one thing that i  wish i'd known at the start of my quite  a long time as health secretary it is  that you can't solve problems with money  alone money matters but there is a big  and legitimate debate about funding for  the nhs and care system  but you can't put three billion pounds  into the system and expect to see change  unless there are three billion pounds  worth of extra doctors and nurses and  beds to use for many families things are  really really difficult the government  talks about the support that it's  already put her in  but do you feel  that more needs to be done to help those  particular on the lowest incomes  i think the government is doing a lot i  mean the sums of money in terms of  billions of pounds which of course come  from taxpayers ultimately are  huge  but the core problem  is that inflation is at record levels  and that is just eating away at family  finances and and we heard the figures  this week that the inflation rate is  even higher for the poorest families so  i think the heart of the issue is how do  we bring down the inflation rate  personally i'd be very attracted to  things that reduce people's fuel bills  because that helps people on low incomes  but also brings down the inflation rate  and some of your colleagues have talked  about um help on universal credit and  obviously there was a 20 pound uplift  during the pandemic is that the kind of  thing you'd like to see targeted at the  people on lower incomes i wouldn't like  to second guess the chance on the  precise measures but i do think  targeting help is the most important  thing do you like the idea of a windfall  tax  i  let me say this i'm pleased that the  chancellor said all options are on the  table  because i find it very difficult to  justify  bp  buying back two and a half billion  pounds of shares because of a hike in  the oil price do you think it's  inevitable that the government's going  to u-turn on this  i think that we are hearing signs that  all options are on the table and so we  need to watch this space it feels like  it's something that you would be quite  attracted to  personally i find it very difficult to  justify those those extra profits  and the met police have concluded their  investigation into partygate 126 fines  for eight gatherings  should boris johnson have done the same  as kier stamron to pledge to resign uh  if he's found guilty of a criminal  offence  i wouldn't want to minimize uh the anger  people feel about the fact that people  in downing street did not follow the  rules that they set for other people  but i think  that the anger that the electorate feel  the way that my party suffered in the  local elections is about more than that  i think the  fundamental  problems in the economy high inflation  low growth are something that are  worrying a lot of families  i don't think the next election will be  about partygate i think it will be  fought on which party has the best  long-term plans for the economy for  the nhs where i hope my book will make a  contribution at the same time they know  i've spoken to conservative mps who said  that party gay came up an awful lot on  the doorstep at those local elections  because i guess it's about  what people feel is the character of the  prime minister as well  i think there are some voters who were  extremely angry and there were some  conservative voters who were extremely  angry  but i think those same voters also  look at the situation uh in the world  look at what's happening in ukraine  and see that what's going wrong there is  of a scale with a totally different  magnitude  i personally don't believe we should  have a change in prime minister right  now britain has been the most robust  member of the western alliance  and i think the only person who'd be  pleased if we had a  hiatus of several months in the  leadership of the uk would be vladimir  putin that doesn't mean to say that i  don't recognize the anger that a lot of  people feel um do you think there's been  a bit of a complacency  after the local election results because  they were pretty bad for your party  particularly in the south  let me put it this way i think it would  be wrong to say this is just midterm  blues i think the core reasons that  people vote conservative which is  the prospect of strong economic growth  the prospect of lower taxes  these things are not true at the moment  and people are really worried about the  the inflation level the  very low growth rates i'm worried about  it because i want us to be able to fund  the nhs spend more on defense and offer  the prospect of lower taxes and the only  way you can do all those things is by  getting growth into the economy  i'm not going to ask the um  the typical if the ball came loose at  the back of the scrum question because  frankly we actually do know the answer  to it you haven't ruled out another  crack at the leadership and you said  earlier this year that your ambition  hasn't completely vanished  why do you think you would be good at  the top job  it's a very good way of asking the same  question sophie um so full matters for  trying um my i've been very honest that  um i don't rule back a return to  frontline politics my ambition has ebbed  somewhat since stepping back because  i've loved uh  being with my family i know it's a  terrible cliche when politicians say  that but it is actually true in my case  it's not disappeared  it hasn't disappeared but i don't think  now is the moment for a change in prime  minister  for the reason i mentioned i think  something that makes vladimir putin  happy is precisely what we don't want at  this moment when we have the first  european war  in our lifetimes the risk of chemical  and biological and even nuclear weapons  being used  the reports that you send back from  places like mario paul i think do  give us a perspective and britain's role  in the western alliance contrary to what  many people thought has been extremely  important very robust and it's what we  need to see now okay thank you very much  thank you

